bool target;

property MaxTarget = Pmax(<> target);
property MinTarget = Pmin(<> target);

const int AMax = 3;

// This showcases the possibility of making the abstract state space infinite even if the concrete one isn't,
// At least with EXPL
// Not as important, but PRED can reduce this
// In practice: the first iteration of BT is finite, it refines with target+counter, and it becomes infinite
// MENU happens to be more lucky, as it introduces all three variables after the first iteration, making it finite
// but this really is pure luck: the current implementation does not prioritize between refinable nodes, just takes the
// first one in the node list, and refines that one. If we took the last one, it would only refine with counter_0. Moreover,
// we currently do not use interpolation, but directly refine with every variable found in the guard or the WP,
// so even with the other refinable node, if we used an interpolating solver, it could have resulted in only tracking counter
// without A
// PRED can easily solve this task with every implemented abstraction type
process ExplInfinite() {
    int counter = 0;
    int(0..AMax) A;
    {= A = DiscreteUniform(0, AMax) =};
    do {
        :: when(counter < A) {= counter++ =}
        :: when(counter == A) break
    };
    {= target = counter == 0 =}
}

ExplInfinite()